From bfa6b49379c0b6db2b78dfa77076385668e52894 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 20:51:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] goose run --debug: Goose backtraces on a crash or an abort

Under goose run --debug, TinyCC compiles the C with a line table
(-bt64 -g1dwarf), which the #line directives make a table of Goose lines,
and the compiler installs a backtrace callback (tcc_set_backtrace_func).
An abort prints, after its message, the Goose frames that led to it,
innermost first; a crash in code TinyCC compiled prints a message and the
same frames:

    goose runtime error: index 3 out of bounds (length 3) (deep.goose:5)
      at leaf (deep.goose:5)
      at middle (deep.goose:9)
      at outer (deep.goose:16)
      at main (deep.goose:23)

Only frames with a .goose line are shown, and the innermost one if it is
C of the user's own (--include); the runtime is left out. C names show as
Goose names: area_g and area_g_2 as area, image_Pixel_g5 as image::Pixel.
An abort's first line is unchanged; the frames come after it.

A crash reaches TinyCC's signal handler behind the runtime's own, which
passes on what is not a data stack's fault. It exits 255 (TinyCC's
rt_exit) where the process died by the signal before, and the handlers
stay installed in the compiler after the run. An abort walks the frame
pointers from the hosted runtime, which is now built with them kept
(-fno-omit-frame-pointer, for Linux; Apple's ABIs keep them anyway). A
fault inside a leaf C function of the host's, such as memcpy, loses the
innermost Goose frame, whose return address is in a register.

TinyCC's backtraces are now on except on Windows, whose PE target links
bt-log.o into every in-memory run with them. Three local TinyCC changes,
recorded in its README: the __rt_info block is added under -nostdlib for
an in-memory run; a frame's file name may be 1023 bytes rather than 99,
as #line names absolute paths; and the DWARF lookup charges an address to
the file of its own row, not to the next row's, which put a return
address at a function's end in the .c file. Without --debug nothing
changes.

Test, on a program whose leaf() indexes out of bounds three calls below
main:

    goose run --debug prog.goose

prints the abort's message and one `at` line per Goose frame.
